codex
The busy-session button implementation has several functional issues: Slack controls are not actually rendered, some stale controls can remain active, interrupt taps can lose earlier follow-ups, and documented configuration/Telegram callback limits are not handled. These are user-visible correctness problems in the new feature.

Full review comments:

- [P2] Render Slack buttons when attaching controls — /Users/wondermonkey/.hermes/hermes-agent-buttons/gateway/platforms/slack.py:3032-3037
  For Slack, anchoring controls to the busy ack/tool bubble currently returns True after only updating `_busy_session_button_map`; no `chat_update` with the Block Kit actions is sent, and `edit_message()` does not consult this map. As a result Slack users never see the Steer/Interrupt/Stop buttons for the normal ack/tool-bubble path, and later cleanup operates on this recorded id despite no controls having been rendered.

- [P2] Preserve all follow-ups on interrupt taps — /Users/wondermonkey/.hermes/hermes-agent-buttons/gateway/run.py:2457-2460
  When multiple follow-ups arrive and the user taps Interrupt, `joined_text` contains all of them, but the adapter pending slot still contains only the latest `MessageEvent` because the busy queue path replaces text by default. The post-run drain prefers that pending event over the agent's `interrupt_message`, so the earlier follow-ups passed to `interrupt()` here are discarded and the next turn only sees the last message.

- [P2] Keep sources until stale controls are cleared — /Users/wondermonkey/.hermes/hermes-agent-buttons/gateway/run.py:2322-2323
  If a halt phrase arrives after controls were already attached, this removes `_pending_followups` before `_clear_busy_session_controls()` runs. Telegram and Discord resolve the chat id for `clear_busy_session_buttons()` from that follow-up list, so cleanup can no longer edit the old anchor and the runner has already popped its ids, leaving stale buttons active in chat; the button-tap path has the same ordering issue.

- [P2] Track all ack anchors for cleanup — /Users/wondermonkey/.hermes/hermes-agent-buttons/gateway/run.py:2407-2409
  For long-running tasks, a second busy follow-up after the ack cooldown sends another ack with buttons and overwrites the only tracked ack id here. End-of-turn cleanup then removes controls from only the latest ack, leaving earlier Telegram/Discord ack buttons active indefinitely and able to dispatch against the same session key later.

- [P2] Honor the documented busy_buttons config — /Users/wondermonkey/.hermes/hermes-agent-buttons/gateway/platforms/telegram.py:3661-3664
  The example config documents `display.busy_buttons: false`, but this helper only reads `HERMES_GATEWAY_BUSY_BUTTONS` and the gateway config bridge never sets that env var. In a normal config-only deployment, setting `display.busy_buttons: false` still returns True here (and in the copied Discord/Slack helpers), so the advertised per-bot disable switch is ignored.

- [P2] Keep Telegram callback data under 64 bytes — /Users/wondermonkey/.hermes/hermes-agent-buttons/gateway/busy_session_buttons.py:89-93
  Telegram limits inline button `callback_data` to 64 bytes, but this embeds the full session key. Real Telegram session keys can exceed the cap for group/forum sessions with long chat/thread/user ids (especially with per-user thread isolation), causing Telegram to reject the keyboard and the busy controls not to render in those chats.
The busy-session button implementation has several functional issues: Slack controls are not actually rendered, some stale controls can remain active, interrupt taps can lose earlier follow-ups, and documented configuration/Telegram callback limits are not handled. These are user-visible correctness problems in the new feature.

Full review comments:

- [P2] Render Slack buttons when attaching controls — /Users/wondermonkey/.hermes/hermes-agent-buttons/gateway/platforms/slack.py:3032-3037
  For Slack, anchoring controls to the busy ack/tool bubble currently returns True after only updating `_busy_session_button_map`; no `chat_update` with the Block Kit actions is sent, and `edit_message()` does not consult this map. As a result Slack users never see the Steer/Interrupt/Stop buttons for the normal ack/tool-bubble path, and later cleanup operates on this recorded id despite no controls having been rendered.

- [P2] Preserve all follow-ups on interrupt taps — /Users/wondermonkey/.hermes/hermes-agent-buttons/gateway/run.py:2457-2460
  When multiple follow-ups arrive and the user taps Interrupt, `joined_text` contains all of them, but the adapter pending slot still contains only the latest `MessageEvent` because the busy queue path replaces text by default. The post-run drain prefers that pending event over the agent's `interrupt_message`, so the earlier follow-ups passed to `interrupt()` here are discarded and the next turn only sees the last message.

- [P2] Keep sources until stale controls are cleared — /Users/wondermonkey/.hermes/hermes-agent-buttons/gateway/run.py:2322-2323
  If a halt phrase arrives after controls were already attached, this removes `_pending_followups` before `_clear_busy_session_controls()` runs. Telegram and Discord resolve the chat id for `clear_busy_session_buttons()` from that follow-up list, so cleanup can no longer edit the old anchor and the runner has already popped its ids, leaving stale buttons active in chat; the button-tap path has the same ordering issue.

- [P2] Track all ack anchors for cleanup — /Users/wondermonkey/.hermes/hermes-agent-buttons/gateway/run.py:2407-2409
  For long-running tasks, a second busy follow-up after the ack cooldown sends another ack with buttons and overwrites the only tracked ack id here. End-of-turn cleanup then removes controls from only the latest ack, leaving earlier Telegram/Discord ack buttons active indefinitely and able to dispatch against the same session key later.

- [P2] Honor the documented busy_buttons config — /Users/wondermonkey/.hermes/hermes-agent-buttons/gateway/platforms/telegram.py:3661-3664
  The example config documents `display.busy_buttons: false`, but this helper only reads `HERMES_GATEWAY_BUSY_BUTTONS` and the gateway config bridge never sets that env var. In a normal config-only deployment, setting `display.busy_buttons: false` still returns True here (and in the copied Discord/Slack helpers), so the advertised per-bot disable switch is ignored.

- [P2] Keep Telegram callback data under 64 bytes — /Users/wondermonkey/.hermes/hermes-agent-buttons/gateway/busy_session_buttons.py:89-93
  Telegram limits inline button `callback_data` to 64 bytes, but this embeds the full session key. Real Telegram session keys can exceed the cap for group/forum sessions with long chat/thread/user ids (especially with per-user thread isolation), causing Telegram to reject the keyboard and the busy controls not to render in those chats.
